message.content:
Below is a quick “starter‑kit” on how to grab a **reactive** queue called *taskQueue* while telling Redisson to serialize/deserialize its elements with your own *JSON* codec (`jsonCodec`).  
I’ll cover two common ways to set up the codec – either globally when building the client or per‑queue – and then show the exact method call you’d make inside your application code.

---

### 1️⃣ Install the right dependencies  

If you’re using Maven / Gradle, make sure you pull in the *redisson‑rx* (or *redisson‑reactive*) artifact along with whatever JSON library you prefer (e.g. Jackson).

#### Maven
```xml
<!-- Core Redisson -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.redisson</groupId>
    <artifactId>redisson</artifactId>
    <version>${redisson.version}</version>
</dependency>

<!-- Reactive extension (uses RxJava2) -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.redisson</groupId>
    <artifactId>redisson-rx</artifactId>
    <version>${redisson.version}</version>
</dependency>

<!-- Optional: Jackson codec helper -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.redisson</groupId>
    <artifactId>redisson-jackson-codec</artifactId>
    <version>${redisson.version}</version>
</dependency>
```

Replace `${redisson.version}` with the latest release (currently 3.x).

---

### 2️⃣ Pick a Codec implementation

| Library | Class |
|---------|-------|
| Jackson | `org.redisson.codec.JacksonCodec` |
| Gson   | `org.redisson.codec.GsonCodec` |

For illustration we’ll use Jackson:

```java
import org.redisson.codec.JacksonCodec;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.core.type.TypeReference;

// Your domain class
public static class Task {
    public String id;
    public String payload;
}
```

Create one shared instance so you don’t keep allocating new codecs:

```java
private static final JacksonCodec TASK_CODEC =
        new JacksonCodec();          // uses default ObjectMapper
// OR customize:
// private static final JacksonCodec TASK_CODEC =
//         new JacksonCodec(new ObjectMapper().configure(...));
```

---

### 3️⃣ Build the **reactive** Redisson client

There are three options:

| Option | When to use | Code |
|--------|-------------|------|
| **Global codec** | All data structures share same codec | `client.getConfig().setCodec(TASK_CODEC);` |
| **Per‑queue codec** | Only specific queues use JSON | Pass codec into `getBlockingDeque()` |
| **Mixed** | Some global defaults + overrides | Combine above |

Here’s the most flexible approach—global defaults plus per‑queue override:

```java
import org.redisson.Redisson;
import org.redisson.api.RBlockingDequeReactive;
import org.redisson.config.Config;
import io.reactivex.Single;      // If you’re using RxJava2

// 1. Configure connection(s)
Config cfg = new Config();
cfg.useSingleServer()
   .setAddress("redis://127.0.0.1:6379");

// 2. Set a fallback codec (optional)
// cfg.setCodec(new MsgPackCodec());     // e.g. binary fallback

// 3. Instantiate the reactive client
var client = Redisson.createReactive(cfg);
```

---

### 4️⃣ Grab the reactive queue

Now you simply ask the client for the queue, passing the name and the codec you created earlier:

```java
String QUEUE_NAME = "taskQueue";

// Using RxJava Single/Observable style
RBlockingDequeReactive<Task> taskQueue =
        client.getBlockingDeque(QUEUE_NAME, TASK_CODEC);

// Example operations
// Push a task onto the tail
Single<Boolean> offerResult = taskQueue.offerLast(
        new Task() {{ id="123"; payload="{\"foo\":\"bar\"}"; }});

// Pop from head (blocks until available)
Single<java.util.Optional<Task>> popResult = taskQueue.takeFirst();

// Subscribe to results (if you’re using RxJava)
offerResult.subscribe(success -> System.out.println("Enqueued: "+success),
                      Throwable::printStackTrace);

popResult.flatMap(opt ->
        opt.map(t -> t.id)
           .map(id -> Single.just(id))
           .orElse(Single.error(new RuntimeException("Empty!")))
).subscribe(System.out::println,
             Throwable::printStackTrace);
```

> **Tip:** If you’re working in a Spring Boot project, consider wiring the `RedissonReactiveClient` as a bean so you can inject it wherever needed.

---

### 5️⃣ Full minimal example (plain Java)

Putting everything together:

```java
package demo;

import org.redisson.Redisson;
import org.redisson.api.RBlockingDequeReactive;
import org.redisson.client.codec.Codec;
import org.redisson.codec.JacksonCodec;
import org.redisson.config.Config;
import io.reactivex.Single;

public class Demo {

    private static final Codec TASK_CODEC = new JacksonCodec();

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Config cfg = new Config();
        cfg.useSingleServer().setAddress("redis://localhost:6379");

        var client = Redisson.createReactive(cfg);

        RBlockingDequeReactive<MyTask> queue =
                client.getBlockingDeque("taskQueue", TASK_CODEC);

        MyTask t = new MyTask("42", "{\"msg\": \"hello\"}");

        // Enqueue
        queue.offerLast(t)
              .doOnSuccess(b -> System.out.println("Queued"))
              .block();            // block here only for demo purposes

        // Dequeue
        java.util.Optional<MyTask> poppedOpt = 
                queue.takeFirst().toFuture().join();

        poppedOpt.ifPresent(popped ->
                System.out.printf("Got %s%n", popped.payload));

        client.shutdown();
    }

    public record MyTask(String id, String payload){}
}
```

Run the app once Redis is running locally, and you will see the enqueue/dequeue logs.

---

### Recap & Checklist

✅ **Dependencies** – `redisson`, `redisson-rx`, optional codec helpers.  
✅ **Codec** – create a single reusable instance (`new JacksonCodec()`) or supply a custom mapper.  
✅ **Client** – build a `RedissonReactiveClient` with desired configuration.  
✅ **Get Queue** – `client.getBlockingDeque("taskQueue", YOUR_CODEC)` returns an `RBlockingDequeReactive<T>` ready for RxJava streams.  
✅ **Use** – Offer/poll/reactively as shown.

That’s all you need to wire up a reactive queue backed by JSON serialization in Redisson. Happy coding!

